Imports:
  - Types:
      - LazyLoader
    From: salt/loader

Annotations: |
  This cell documents the two long-running daemon entry points at the root of the salt package
  — minion.py's `Minion`/`SMinion` and master.py's `Master`/`MWorker` — not the whole salt/
  directory (every subdirectory documented elsewhere in this forest has its own cell). These
  classes are the composition root of the entire architecture: at startup, each one calls the
  salt/loader factory routines (minion_mods, states, runner, returners, grains,
  utils, cache, ...) to build every `LazyLoader` the rest of this forest describes, and holds
  the results as its own attributes for the lifetime of the process.

---

"Minion(opts: dict[str, object], timeout: int, safe: bool, loaded_base_name: str | None, io_loop: object | None, jid_queue: list[str] | None, load_grains: bool)":
  location: minion.py
  annotations: |
    Instantiates a running minion: establishes the transport connection to its master(s), loads
    every plugin category via salt/loader, and runs the main event loop that receives and
    executes published jobs.

    `opts`: the Salt minion options dictionary
    `timeout`: connection/auth timeout in seconds
    `safe`: when true, avoid destructive re-initialization on connection failure
    `load_grains`: compute grains during construction (skipped when a caller supplies them
    separately)
  properties:
    "functions -> LazyLoader": |
      The execution-modules `LazyLoader` built by salt.loader.minion_mods, bound as __salt__
      for every job this minion executes.
    "returners -> LazyLoader": |
      The returners `LazyLoader` built by salt.loader.returners, used to publish job results
      back.
    "states -> LazyLoader": |
      The state-modules `LazyLoader` built by salt.loader.states, used when this minion runs
      state.apply/state.highstate.
  methods:
    "_load_modules(force_refresh: bool, notify: bool, grains: dict[str, object] | None)": |
      (Re)build functions/returners/states/utils/serializers/matchers/executors from
      salt/loader, e.g. after a saltutil.sync_all or scheduled module refresh.

      `force_refresh`: bypass any grains cache while reloading
      `notify`: fire a module-refresh-complete event once done
      grains: reuse this pre-computed grains dict instead of recomputing it
    "sync_connect_master(timeout: int | None, failed: bool)": |
      Block until the transport connection to this minion's master is established (or `timeout`
      elapses), used during startup before tune_in begins processing jobs.
    "tune_in(start: bool)": |
      The minion's main event loop: listens for published jobs on the transport and, for each one,
      dispatches it against functions and returns the result through returners. Runs until the
      process is stopped.

"SMinion(opts: dict[str, object], context: dict[str, object] | None)":
  location: minion.py
  annotations: |
    A fully loaded minion with no live connection to a master — every plugin-category
    `LazyLoader` (functions, states, returners, utils, ...) is built exactly as `Minion`
    builds them, but no transport is opened. Used by masterless execution paths (`salt-call
    --local`) and internally wherever code needs "a minion's full function set" without running an
    actual minion daemon.
  properties:
    "functions -> LazyLoader": |
      The execution-modules `LazyLoader`, identical in construction to Minion.functions.
    "states -> LazyLoader": |
      The state-modules `LazyLoader`, identical in construction to Minion.states.

"Master(opts: dict[str, object])":
  location: master.py
  annotations: |
    The salt-master server process: validates/generates its signing key, then spawns the
    `MWorker` pool, event monitor, fileserver-update, and maintenance subprocesses that together
    handle minion authentication, published commands, and job returns.
  methods:
    "read_or_generate_key(remove: bool, fs_wait: float)": |
      Load the master's signing key from disk, generating a new one first if `remove` is set or
      none exists yet.
    "start()": |
      Perform pre-flight checks, then fork and start every master subprocess (`MWorker` pool,
      Maintenance, FileserverUpdate, EventMonitor, RequestServer), blocking until the
      master is shut down.

"MWorker(opts: dict[str, object], mkey: object, key: object, req_channels: list[object], pool_name: str | None, pool_index: int | None)":
  location: master.py
  annotations: |
    One worker process in the master's process pool; handles the backend operations for incoming
    encrypted (AESFuncs) and clear (ClearFuncs) requests, including dispatching runner/wheel
    commands and processing minion job returns via returners.

    `mkey`, `key`: the master's signing/encryption key material, shared across the worker pool
    req_channels: transport channels this worker reads incoming requests from
  methods:
    "run()": |
      The worker process's main loop: read a request from req_channels, route it to the
      appropriate handler (AESFuncs/AuthFuncs/ClearFuncs) based on its encryption/auth
      status, and write back the response.

